The paper under review sits between the ViT Registers anchor (8, 8, 8, 8 — oral) and the SPiT paper (6, 8, 5, 3 — rejected). Compared to ViT Registers, this paper has a cleaner conceptual framing and broader case studies, but a significant methodological confound (sequence length vs. locality) that the Registers paper avoids. Compared to ChannelViT (avg ~6.5, poster), this paper has a more fundamental finding but a more serious confound issue.

The core "locality is not necessary" claim is well-supported and the two-trends analysis is genuinely insightful. The sequence length confound is real but does not invalidate the existence-proof argument — it only weakens the attributional ("why it's better") claim. The DiT latent-space inconsistency and missing resolution disclosure are real but correctable.

Positioning: Solidly in poster territory, above the SPiT/rejected papers, below the Registers/oral tier. The center of the cluster for papers with this profile (interesting finding, one real methodological gap, honest scope) is around **6.5**.

